#14. A disease affects 2% of a population. A medical test correctly identifies a person with the disease 95% of the time. The test also produces a positive result for 5% of people who do not have the disease. Write a Python program to apply Bayes’ theorem and calculate the probability that a person actually has the disease given that the test result is positive. Use Matplotlib to compare the prior probability of having the disease with the posterior probability after a positive test result.

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

prior = 0.02
sensitivity = 0.95
false_positive = 0.05

posterior = (sensitivity * prior) / (
    sensitivity * prior +
    false_positive * (1 - prior)
)

print("Prior Probability:", prior)
print("Posterior Probability:", posterior)

plt.bar(["Prior", "After Positive Test"],
        [prior, posterior])
plt.ylabel("Probability")
plt.title("Bayes' Theorem: Disease Prediction")
plt.show()
